th all the other virtues and  vices it's possible to go too far  it's possible to be lacking the  excess in this case is  boastfulness or being a braggart  these days we would call it inflating  your qualities  there was a very interesting discussion  a while ago in which i  participated a bit about whether one  should be a self-promoter  or not and i'll touch on that at the end  a lot of times we think of people being  self-promoting is falling over here  on the other side of vice or deficiency  we have  those who engage in self-deprecation  the greek word for that is actually  irony so  socrates is a representative of the  ironic  attitude towards things and now irony  can mean something more where  you're saying something and you're  trying to lead the interlocutor to  realize something about themselves  you know for example when he says to  many of his interlockers  well you know you obviously know all  about that could you enlighten me  but there's also a bit of uh  putting the self down involved in that  well i don't really know about anything  so why don't you enlighten me  it's kind of a um at least in socrates  part  it's kind of a pretense in many cases  and and many people  felt that he was sort of not  giving them the straight story and they  would say well you know come out and  actually say what you mean and so i  just prefer to you know engage in this  question and dialogue and stuff like  that  aristotle says that a lot of people who  do that are  actually doing something that's that's  vicious he thinks that this  vice is not as bad as this vice and that  actually the virtue  is going to steer over this way  oftentimes we look at people like this  and we say that while they're being  humble they are  they're fitting in well with other  people by not  pushing their own qualities their own  achievements by not making a big  big show of themselves people like this  are a bit easier to get along with  than people like this although this  could go too far  if you think aristotle hasn't talk about  this so much  but this is a very common problem in our  society people who  can't take a compliment who don't know  what to do  with a compliment who get all flustered  and then have to deny  what they're being complimented about  that that's a problem  you know not being able to take  criticism is one problem not being able  to take praise  is another problem and that would fall  over over here  by the way people over here are going to  be very bad about taking criticism  so here's what's really interesting  about this  aristotle says that  the truthful person is a lover of truth  feel alethea they they love truth  and that means loving something  outside of oneself that puts the self  into  context that's what allows the truthful  person to actually  be truthful about themselves they  they prefer to be  in the truth than to think false things  about themselves  so when they do something wrong or  really not up to standards  they are okay not with having done that  but with it being said that that's the  case  without being represented they are able  to take criticism  because they actually do take criticism  for what it  what it means the truth in it  it doesn't mean that they're gonna  they're gonna accept any sort of  criticism off-base criticism they're  probably not gonna accept that  this person over here is a lover of  falsehood  they love a false inflated  image of themselves that they want to  hold up  in front of other people and also  for themselves  aristotle says something really  interesting he says  those who boast for the sake of  reputation pretend to possess such  qualities as they're crazy  as an admired those who possess  those who do so for profit pretend to  accomplishments that are useful to their  fellows and can be counterfeited without  detection  for instance where he says something  really funny proficiency in prophecy  anybody can claim to be a prophet or a  fortune teller or  a success guru 